          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What feature, in addition to regular uterine contractions before 37 weeks, is re</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What feature, in addition to regular uterine contractions before 37 weeks, is required to diagnose preterm labour?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Positive fetal fibronectin test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fetal fibronectin is a predictive biomarker used in risk stratification, but it is not part of the diagnostic definition of preterm labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Documented cervical change (dilatation or effacement)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Preterm labour is defined as regular contractions (≥4 per 20 minutes or ≥8 per hour) with documented cervical dilatation or effacement before 37 completed weeks.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rupture of membranes confirmed on speculum examination</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Membrane rupture defines PROM, not preterm labour. The two conditions often coexist but are diagnosed by different criteria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fetal heart rate decelerations on cardiotocography</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CTG decelerations suggest fetal compromise, which is a separate concern. The diagnosis of preterm labour rests on contractions plus cervical change, not fetal heart rate patterns.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key criterion is documented cervical change. Regular contractions alone are not sufficient — Braxton-Hicks and uterine irritability are common in the third trimester and do not constitute preterm labour."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Without documented cervical change, even frequent contractions may represent uterine irritability or Braxton-Hicks — not true preterm labour."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
