          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A primigravida at 39 weeks presents with confirmed rupture of membranes for 20 h</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A primigravida at 39 weeks presents with confirmed rupture of membranes for 20 hours but no contractions. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer betamethasone and continue expectant management</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antenatal corticosteroids are indicated between 24 and 34 weeks for fetal lung maturity. At 39 weeks the lungs are mature; steroids serve no purpose here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Offer induction of labour</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In term PROM, if spontaneous labour has not begun, induction is offered within 12–24 hours because the risk of chorioamnionitis rises significantly with prolonged membrane rupture.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Discharge with advice to return when contractions begin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Prolonged membrane rupture at term significantly increases the risk of ascending infection and chorioamnionitis. Expectant management beyond 24 hours is not recommended.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proceed directly to emergency caesarean section</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>There is no indication for emergency caesarean section in uncomplicated term PROM. Vaginal delivery via induction should be attempted first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In term PROM (≥37 weeks), induction of labour is offered within 12–24 hours if spontaneous labour has not started, because the risk of chorioamnionitis rises with time."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The principle is that the closer to term, the stronger the indication to deliver — and at 39 weeks with prolonged ROM, induction is the standard of care."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
