          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the Respectful Maternity Care framework, the Bohren typology (2015) primarily</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the Respectful Maternity Care framework, the Bohren typology (2015) primarily provides which of the following?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A classification system for identifying forms of mistreatment during childbirth</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The Bohren typology serves as a diagnostic lens — it classifies mistreatment into categories such as physical abuse, verbal abuse, stigma, and failure to meet professional standards of care.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A charter of women's entitlements during labour and childbirth</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Entitlements are defined by the White Ribbon Alliance (WRA) Respectful Maternity Care Charter (2011). The Bohren typology complements it by classifying how those entitlements are violated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clinical guidelines for managing complications in labour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Bohren typology addresses systemic and interpersonal mistreatment, not clinical management of obstetric complications. It is a rights-based framework, not a clinical protocol.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Minimum infrastructure standards for labour rooms in district hospitals</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Infrastructure standards are facility-level requirements. The Bohren typology focuses on how women are treated — behaviour, communication, and systemic failures — not the physical facility.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The Bohren typology (2015) is a classification of mistreatment during childbirth. It complements the WRA Charter — the charter defines rights, and the typology identifies how those rights are breached."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The WRA Charter sets the normative standard (what women are entitled to), while the Bohren typology provides the diagnostic lens (how those entitlements are violated in practice)."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
